What:           /sys/class/qcom-battery/parts_charge_pause
Date:           October 2026
Contact:        Lufrz
Description:
                Experimental, default-off battery charging pause for Xiaomi
                Parts. Write 1 to request FCC=0; write 0 to restore. Reads are
                off, active, or restore_pending. Active means a zero FCC was
                acknowledged and read back, NOT confirmed electrical isolation
                of the battery. Only wired USB charging is supported.

                While active, battery/status reports Not charging instead of
                firmware Charging or Full. Discharging and error states are
                preserved. Session transitions notify power_supply consumers.
                Firmware readback is allowed to settle for up to ten queries
                spaced 100 ms apart, requiring two consecutive matching values.
                Transport timeouts can add latency. An unconfirmed zero write
                is explicitly cancelled if the old FCC is still reported; it
                must not silently leave a delayed zero request outstanding.

                Enabling requires good health, 30-95% capacity, 20-40 degrees C,
                USB online, and a positive, readable FCC to restore. Repeated
                enabling does not extend a session. Unplugging, invalid readings,
                a GLINK restart, or a 30-minute boottime deadline ends a session.
                Checks run every 5 seconds while CPUs are awake, without holding
                a wake lock; elapsed sleep is included in the next deadline check.
                Thermal/voltage protections and USB input controls are unchanged.

                Ordinary FCC writers take precedence. A different nonzero FCC
                from firmware is never overwritten. Failed or uncertain writes
                retain restoration state; restore_pending retries automatically
                and also accepts explicit 0. Normal shutdown attempts restoration.
                No state survives reboot. Forced resets, firmware failures, or a
                firmware policy which ignores FCC=0 cannot guarantee restoration
                or charge bypass. Restore explicitly before rebooting or flashing.
